<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Léger-Félicité Sonthonax</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1763">1763</a>–<a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Sonthonax</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Léger-Félicité Sonthonax was a French politician and colonial administrator who, as Civil Commissioner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>, proclaimed the freedom of all slaves in its north province on 29 August 1793. A lawyer from Oyonnax and a member of the Society of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a> of the Blacks, he was sent to the colony in 1792 with Étienne Polverel to enforce equal rights for free people of colour, not to end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> ratified emancipation on 4 February 1794, applying it to all French colonies. Acquitted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1795, he returned to Saint-Domingue, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a> sent him back to France in 1797; he died in Oyonnax in 1813.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The National Convention's decree of 15 May 1792 restored the rights of the free people of colour; <b>Léger-Félicité Sonthonax</b>, Civil Commissioner and de facto ruler from September 1792 to 1795, stripped the white Creoles of military power and abolished slavery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The commissioner <b>Sonthonax</b> abolished slavery in the north in August 1793, the National Convention in all French colonies on 4 February 1794, and after Napoleon’s attempt to reconquer the colony Haiti declared its independence on 1 January 1804.</blockquote><a href="/societies/saint-domingue">Social Hierarchy of Saint-Domingue</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oyonnax, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Oyonnax, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.26,5.66&amp;year=1795" aria-label="Oyonnax on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4683 536 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Léger-Félicité Sonthonax"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5133 806h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5133 806h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.26,5.66&amp;year=1795">What was here in 1795? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
